 Coarse-Fine Sampling is a two-pass rendering technique for Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) that improves image quality and sampling efficiency by first estimating the scene's density distribution with a coarse pass, then using that distribution to importance-sample a fine pass. It sets itself apart from uniform sampling by concentrating computational effort on high-density regions identified in the first pass, and from single-stage methods by decoupling the rough density estimation from the detailed color/density evaluation.
 
-### Base-NeRF Sampling & Architecture
-Base-NeRF uses a specific architecture with hash grids and MLPs for its proposal networks [S3]. Proposal 1 queries 128 linearly spaced samples [S3]. Proposal 2 queries 96 samples using probability density sampling [S3]. The final NeRF model queries 48 samples using probability density sampling, increased to 192 points at test time for higher quality [S3]. The architecture uses Hash Grids with parameters (levels/log2 size/base/max) and MLPs (layers/dimension) [S3]. Proposal 1: Hash Grid 5/17/16/64, MLP 1/16 [S3]. Proposal 2: Hash Grid 5/17/16/256, MLP 1/16 [S3]. Positional embedding uses standard encoding, directional embedding uses spherical harmonics [S3]. Sigma MLP: 1/64. Color MLP: 2/64 [S3].
-
-### Speedup via importance thresholding
-This variant prunes rays corresponding to background regions based on an importance threshold [S4, unofficial]. On the Blender dataset, this yields large speedups because many pixels correspond to the background [S4, unofficial]. NeRF-ID renders views up to 50% faster while achieving better results than NeRF† [S4, unofficial].
-
-### HeadNeRF Volume Rendering
-A volume rendering strategy that computes a low-resolution 2D feature map IF from ray samples to serve as input for a subsequent 2D neural rendering module [S5]. Number of points sampled per ray: 64 [S5]. Output feature map IF dimensions: $R^{256 \times 32 \times 32}$ [S5]. Hierarchical volume sampling is removed to speed up inference, accepting potential quality trade-offs for real-time performance [S5].
-
-### Two-Phase Patch Selection Method
-A method to balance appearance and geometric continuity in NeRF synthesis by searching patches in two phases, significantly enhancing speed [S6]. In the first phase, search patches for density only [S6]. Speedup comparison: For 100^2 size, Baseline 163.47s vs Two-phase 14.99s (10.90x); for 200^2, 270.70s vs 23.62s (11.46x); for 300^2, 454.41s vs 38.46s (11.82x); for 400^2, 714.64s vs 59.57s (12.00x) [S6].
+### [Semantic Loss Computation](15616-semantic-loss-computation.md)
+Semantic Loss Computation computes the cross-entropy loss over coarse and fine semantic predictions for query rays to train a generalizable semantic field. It differs from standard photometric NeRF losses by enforcing semantic consistency across views via ray-based feature projection, allowing the model to learn contextual geometry for semantic information rather than just color. It is a core component of the Two-stage NeRF Rendering pipeline, specifically enabling the training of the semantic head alongside the geometry-aware network.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method reduces the number of network queries compared to uniform sampling of the same total sample count by focusing queries on relevant regions [G]. However, it adds the overhead of the coarse pass [G]. Total queries per ray are typically 256 [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing parameters for both coarse and fine networks [G]. The coarse network is typically smaller [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_c + N_f)$ network queries per ray, where $N_c$ and $N_f$ are the number of coarse and fine samples.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_c + N_f)$ to store intermediate densities and colors.
 *   **Measured Performance:** On an NVIDIA V100, rendering a realistic synthetic dataset (640k rays/image) or real scenes (762k rays/image) takes approximately 30 seconds per frame, resulting in 150-200 million network queries per image [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Significantly improves rendering quality per network query compared to uniform sampling [G]. Allows for efficient training by providing gradient signals to the coarse network [G].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires training two networks (or a multi-stage network) [G]. The coarse pass adds latency if not optimized [G].
-*   **When to use:** When high-quality novel view synthesis is required and computational resources allow for two network evaluations per ray [G].
-*   **When not to use:** For real-time applications where latency is critical and quality can be sacrificed, or for scenes with very simple geometry where uniform sampling suffices [G].
+*   **Strengths:** Significantly reduces the number of network queries required for high-quality images by focusing computation on relevant regions [S1, S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires training two networks (coarse and fine) or a multi-stage proposal system, increasing training complexity [S2, unofficial][S3].
+*   **When to Use:** When rendering high-quality novel views from a trained NeRF is required, and computational resources are limited [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** For real-time applications where the overhead of two-stage sampling is prohibitive, or for scenes with uniform density where uniform sampling is sufficient [S5, S6].
 
 ## Background
-Two-stage NeRF Rendering was introduced in the original NeRF paper by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1]. It was designed to address the high computational cost of volume rendering in neural implicit representations [S1]. The method has since become a standard baseline for NeRF variants, with numerous extensions such as proposal networks [S2, unofficial], hash grid-based accelerations [S3], and importance thresholding [S4, unofficial].
+Two-stage NeRF Rendering was introduced in the original NeRF paper by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1]. It was developed to address the inefficiency of uniform sampling in neural radiance fields, which often wastes queries on empty space. The method has since become a standard component in many NeRF variants, including those using proposal networks [S2, unofficial][S3] and semantic guidance [S1].
 
